<!doctype html>
<html lang="{{ page_language|default('en') }}">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>targum</title>
{% include '_icons.html.j2' %}
<style>{{ asset('reader.css') }}{{ asset('signin.css') }}</style>
{{ hebrew_face() }}
{{ chrome_face() }}
<script>{{ asset('keep.js') }}</script>
</head>
<body class="signin">
<main class="gate">
  {#- The lockup alone. There is no navigation to offer someone who is not in yet, and
      a header with three dead links is worse than no header. -#}
  <a class="brand" href="/" aria-label="targum">
    <svg class="brand-mark" viewBox="20 12 56 72" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
      <rect x="20" y="12" width="22" height="62" rx="5" fill="currentColor"></rect>
      <rect x="54" y="22" width="22" height="62" rx="5" fill="var(--mark-accent)"></rect>
    </svg>
    <span>targum</span>
  </a>

  {#- The door itself is a card on the desk (§13, 2026-09-14): the lockup and the foot
      stand on the ground around it, the way the places and the footer do on every page
      behind it. -#}
  <div class="door">
  {#- What went wrong last time, where something did: a Google sign-in that was
      cancelled, took too long, or was for an address that is not on the guest list. Said
      on the door itself rather than on a page of its own, because the way out of every
      one of them is the form underneath. -#}
  {%- if said %}<p class="said trouble" role="status">{{ said }}</p>{% endif %}
  {% if landing %}
  {#- The link arrived. Nothing has been spent yet: a mail client that fetches this page
      to preview it has only read it, and the account is opened by the button below. -#}
  <h1>{{ t("signin.page.sign-in-as", "Sign in as {address}", address=landing) }}</h1>
  <p class="said">{{ t("signin.page.press-to-sign-in-on-this-browser", "Press to sign in on this browser.") }}</p>
  <form method="post" action="/account/enter">
    <input type="hidden" name="t" value="{{ token }}">
    <button class="go" type="submit">{{ t("signin.page.sign-in", "Sign in") }}</button>
  </form>
  <p class="aside">{{ t("signin.page.didn-t-ask-for-this-you-can", "Didn't ask for this? You can close the page.") }}</p>

  {% elif expired %}
  <h1>{{ t("signin.page.that-link-has-been-used", "That link has been used") }}</h1>
  <p class="said">{{ t("signin.page.each-link-works-once-for-twenty-minutes", "Each link works once, for twenty minutes. We'll send you another.") }}</p>
  <form method="post" action="/account/sign-in" id="ask">
    <label for="email">{{ t("signin.page.email", "Email") }}</label>
    <input type="email" id="email" name="email" required autocomplete="email"
           autocapitalize="off" spellcheck="false" placeholder="{{ t("signin.page.you-example-com", "you@example.com") }}">
    <button class="go" type="submit">{{ t("signin.page.send-a-link", "Send a link") }}</button>
  </form>

  {% else %}
  <h1>{{ t("signin.page.hebrew-with-the-translation-beside-it", "Hebrew, with the translation beside it") }}</h1>
  {#- One door was the rule here, and the reason was good: "Nobody has to work out which
      of two buttons they are." Google is a second door and not a second answer to that
      question — it proves the same address faster, and the account it reaches is the same
      account, because `person` is keyed on the address and nothing else
      (targum-internal#304).

      Drawn only where this install can finish the exchange. No Google script, no widget:
      an anchor to accounts.google.com, which is a link the reader chooses to follow and
      the one thing §11 lets off the page. -#}
  {%- if google %}
  <a class="go with-google" href="/account/google">{{ t("signin.page.continue-with-google", "Continue with Google") }}</a>
  <p class="or">{{ t("signin.page.or", "or") }}</p>
  {%- endif %}
  <p class="said">{{ t("signin.page.no-password-we-ll-email-you-a", "No password. We'll email you a link.") }}</p>
  <form method="post" action="/account/sign-in" id="ask">
    <label for="email">{{ t("signin.page.email", "Email") }}</label>
    <input type="email" id="email" name="email" required autocomplete="email"
           autocapitalize="off" spellcheck="false" placeholder="{{ t("signin.page.you-example-com", "you@example.com") }}">
    <button class="go" type="submit">{{ t("signin.page.send-a-link", "Send a link") }}</button>
  </form>
  <p class="aside">{{ t("signin.page.once-you-re-signed-in-your-words", "Once you're signed in, your words follow you between browsers.") }}</p>
  {% endif %}

  <p class="said sent" id="sent" role="status" hidden></p>
  </div>

  {#- The foot the rest of the site has. Not navigation — there is still nothing to
      navigate to from out here — but somebody handing an address over deserves to read
      what happens to it before they do, which is what the privacy link is for once the
      documents are open. Until then this is the same three links as everywhere else. -#}
  {% include '_public_foot.html.j2' %}
</main>
{% if strings %}<script>window.TARGUM_STRINGS = {{ strings|tojson }};</script>
{% endif %}<script>{{ asset('strings.js') }}</script>
<script>{{ asset('signin.js') }}</script>
</body>
</html>
